===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|roume|lang=fro}}, {{term|reume}}, {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|rume|lang=frm}}, {{term|ryeume}}, and their source, {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term|rheuma}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|ῥεῦμα||stream, humour|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɹuːm/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|-}}

# {{uncountable}} Watery or thin discharge of [[serum]] or [[mucus]], especially from the [[eye]]s or [[nose]], formerly thought to cause disease. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1916''', {{w|James Joyce}}, ''A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man'', Macmillan Press Ltd, 102
#*: He wore about his shoulders a heavy cloak; his pale face was drawn and his voice broken with '''rheum'''.
# [[illness|Illness]] or [[disease]] thought to be caused by such secretions; a [[cold]], [[catarrh]]; [[rheumatism]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1603''', {{w|John Florio}}, translating Michel de Montaigne, ''Essays'', II.8:
#*: he [...] had all his faculties free and easie, onely a '''rheume''' excepted that fell into his stomacke.
# {{poetic}} [[tears|Tears]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}

